    "expect": "60\n200.0\nTrue",
    "practice": "What rate drains an existing backlog under steady conditions?|Service minus arrival|Service plus arrival|Arrival alone|Backlog plus service|Only capacity beyond ongoing arrivals reduces accumulated work.|Summing arrival and service does not represent net backlog reduction.|Arrivals add work rather than describing the spare processing rate.|Backlog is a stock and cannot be added to a rate as a drain rate.\nWhat if service equals arrival?|Ideally unchanged backlog|Backlog necessarily drains|The source becomes empty|Retention becomes infinite|New work consumes all service capacity, leaving no catchup margin.|Equal service and arrivals leave zero positive net drain in the stated ideal model.|The source can continue receiving records while backlog remains stable.|Rate balance does not change log retention duration.\nWhat does backpressure do?|Bounded downstream admission|Create unlimited processing capacity|Delete all source history|Guarantee zero latency|Backpressure controls demand but waiting and buffering still need management.|Admission control cannot create unlimited downstream throughput.|Backpressure regulates work rather than requiring deletion of history.|Controlling overload can increase queueing and does not guarantee zero latency.\nBacklog is 6000 and excess service is 100 per minute. Catchup time?|60 minutes|600 minutes|100 minutes|6 minutes|6000 divided by 100 yields sixty minutes.|Six hundred minutes is ten times the stated backlog divided by excess service.|One hundred is a rate rather than the elapsed catchup duration.|Six minutes would require one thousand excess events per minute.\nA log retains one day but recovery needs two. What is required?|Retention or backup source|Only a renamed checkpoint|Only a shorter trigger interval|Only a fresh consumer group|Required source history must remain available throughout the recovery plan.|Renaming progress does not retain the second day of source payloads.|More frequent work cannot recover records already expired during the outage.|A different group identity does not recreate missing retained history.\nA rate limiter protects the sink but raises source lag. What should monitoring show?|Sink health and source lag|Only sink success|Only source schema|Only worker process count|The tradeoff must be visible so protected resources do not hide retention risk.|Sink success alone can hide increasing source lag near retention expiry.|Schema validity does not show source-history exposure.|Process count does not establish either sink saturation or age of backlog.\nWhy compare lag age as well as offset distance?|Record rates vary over time|Offsets are always wall-clock seconds|Every partition has equal volume|All events arrive in order globally|The same record distance can represent very different elapsed retention exposure.|Offsets count positions rather than a fixed number of wall-clock seconds.|Partition volumes can differ and do not make offset distance a time measure.|Kafka does not guarantee one global event-time arrival order across partitions.",
    "formal": "What is sustainable catchup headroom?|Capacity minus ongoing arrivals|The configured worker count|The arrival rate alone|The current backlog size|Only excess capacity can process backlog while serving new arrivals.|Worker count must be translated into sustainable service capacity and compared with ongoing arrivals.|Arrival rate omits service capacity and cannot establish positive backlog-draining headroom.|Backlog describes accumulated work, not spare sustained processing capacity.\nWhat does source retention bound?|Retained replay-history age|All target commit durations|Business-key uniqueness|Consumer CPU utilization|Replay relies on source records still being retained.|Retention affects source history availability rather than bounding each sink transaction.|Keeping events longer does not establish whether business identities are unique.|CPU usage is a processing measurement rather than a source-history lifetime.\nWhich stage can be the bottleneck?|Any throughput-limiting stage|Only the driver by definition|Only the newest consumer|Only the largest partition count|End-to-end throughput depends on the limiting resource under the workload.|A source, executor or sink can constrain throughput even when driver capacity is sufficient.|Consumer creation time does not identify the stage limiting sustained work.|Partition count alone does not establish actual source, compute or sink throughput.\nA service processes exactly 100 events per second and receives 100. An outage backlog exists. What is needed?|Service above ongoing arrival rate|Only restart at the arrival rate|Only poll the log more frequently|Only create a new group id|At equal rates the backlog persists, so catchup requires capacity above arrivals or lower demand.|Equal service and arrivals leave no net capacity to drain the existing backlog.|More frequent polling cannot exceed the stated sustainable processing capacity.|Changing group identity does not increase end-to-end service capacity.\nA team increases compute but sink throughput stays fixed. What may happen?|A new backlog at the sink|End-to-end capacity always doubles|Every source offset disappears|All replayed events become unique|Accelerating a nonbottleneck stage does not remove a downstream limit.|A fixed sink remains a limiting stage even if upstream compute gets faster.|Increasing compute does not remove source positions or their retention limits.|Capacity changes do not provide identity-aware deduplication.\nA checkpoint points to expired source records. What is the recovery limitation?|Missing payloads remain missing|The checkpoint guarantees all bytes exist|A consumer rename restores the log|A shorter trigger recreates history|Stored positions identify needed records but do not contain unavailable source payloads.|Progress metadata identifies missing history but cannot supply its payload bytes.|A group rename changes consumer identity rather than restoring expired source records.|Faster triggering cannot recreate data that is no longer retained.\nBacklog is 9000; arrival is 200 per minute and service is 350. Ideal catchup time?|60 minutes|25.71 minutes|45 minutes|150 minutes|Excess capacity is 150, so 9000/150=60.|Dividing by total service ignores new arrivals.|Dividing by arrival rate does not measure drain.|One hundred fifty is the excess rate, not time.\nArrival rises from 100 to 180 while service remains 160. How does backlog change per minute?|Grows by 20|Shrinks by 60|Shrinks by 20|Stays fixed|Arrival exceeds service by twenty per minute.|Sixty is the old margin before the rate increase.|The sign is wrong because arrivals are larger.|Equal rates would be required for a steady backlog.\nAn outage lasts 4 hours and catchup needs 3 more; source retention is 6 hours. What planned gap exists under this conservative budget?|1 hour|3 hours|6 hours|13 hours|Outage plus catchup is seven hours, exceeding six by one.|Three is catchup time, not the excess.|Six is available retention.|Adding all values does not measure the shortfall.\nRecovery has 1.2 million queued events, with 5000 new events arriving per minute. A candidate service rate is 15000 per minute, but the recovery obligation allows only 90 minutes to clear the backlog. Which plan meets that obligation under an ideal constant-rate model?|18334/min, or renegotiate with retained replay|15000 per minute because it processes the backlog in eighty minutes|13334 per minute because ongoing arrivals need no reserved capacity|10000 per minute because that exceeds the ongoing arrival rate|The net drain must be at least 1200000/90 = 13333.34 events per minute; adding 5000 continuing arrivals requires at least 18334 integer service capacity. The 15000 proposal needs 120 minutes, so a slower plan requires an explicitly changed recovery contract and sufficient retained history.|Eighty minutes divides backlog by total service while ignoring continuing arrivals.|13334 total leaves only 8334 net drain, insufficient for the ninety-minute obligation.|10000 total leaves 5000 net drain and needs 240 minutes, not ninety.",
