    "expect": "280\n350\nTrue ['A', 'B', 'C']",
    "practice": "Why share metric definitions between live and batch paths?|To reduce semantic drift|To guarantee every source arrives on time|To eliminate all state|To remove reconciliation|Shared versioned rules align meaning, while completeness and recovery still need controls.|Shared rules align calculations but cannot force source arrivals to be timely.|A shared definition does not eliminate stored live or batch state.|Common semantics still require population and output reconciliation.\nWhat should a live incomplete value be labeled?|Provisional|Final by default|Audited automatically|Globally exact forever|Consumers need explicit status when later corrections may change a value.|Incomplete live output should not be called final without its completion obligation.|An audit label requires evidence rather than merely fast availability.|A provisional view can change when late or corrected input arrives.\nHow should a full authoritative correction avoid duplicate effects?|Keyed scoped replacement/merge|Append every replay as a new sale|Assign random identities|Ignore the original source interval|The correction must address the same business population rather than add copies.|Appending each replay can duplicate already represented business events.|Random identities defeat matching against existing business keys.|Ignoring interval scope can change unrelated periods or omit affected data.\nA live total is 280 and final is 350. What delta should reconciliation explain?|70|630|280|350|The correction changes the total by 350-280=70.|Six hundred thirty adds the two totals instead of measuring the correction.|Two hundred eighty is the live amount rather than the final-minus-live delta.|Three hundred fifty is the final amount rather than the change.\nA daily close needs final source completeness. Does low live latency prove that?|No|Yes if triggers are frequent|Yes if worker CPU is low|Yes if the dashboard is current|Latency and completeness are independent obligations.|Frequent triggers can process incomplete source data quickly.|Low CPU does not prove every required source event arrived.|A current dashboard can display a fresh but provisional partial population.\nA corrected generation is exposed without telling export consumers. What remains at risk?|Consumers with superseded data|Only source schema compatibility|Only current pipeline task health|Only the new generation checksum|External consumers may continue using earlier artifacts unless correction semantics are explicit.|A compatible schema does not replace a consumer\u2019s superseded report values.|Green execution does not update previously exported bytes held elsewhere.|A producer checksum identifies new output without proving consumers received the correction.\nThe authoritative correction is applied twice by keyed replacement. What property should hold?|The second application is unchanged|The second application doubles revenue|Every key gains a random suffix|Source retention becomes unlimited|Idempotent replacement preserves the intended corrected state on replay.|Doubling revenue on the second application would violate idempotent replacement.|Random suffixes create new identities rather than replacing the same keyed scope.|Repeated application does not change source retention.",
    "formal": "What is a provisional view?|An explicitly correctable result|A result guaranteed final forever|A source backup by definition|A consumer group assignment|Provisional status communicates that completeness or late-data finalization is pending.|A final-forever guarantee contradicts the explicit possibility of later correction.|A provisional result is a consumer-facing state, not necessarily a backup of raw inputs.|Group assignment is consumption coordination rather than a result\u2019s finality status.\nWhy keep source and rule versions for both paths?|To explain and reproduce differences|To guarantee no data changes ever|To remove all consumer communication|To avoid key identity checks|Lineage identifies whether differences arise from population or transformation versions.|Versions explain changes; they do not prevent new source data or rule changes.|Consumers still need to understand provisional and corrected generations.|Version tracking complements key reconciliation rather than replacing identity tests.\nWhat should final publication validate?|Scope and business invariants|Only the latest live refresh|Only the newest file timestamp|Only a successful worker heartbeat|A final-generation promise needs completeness and correctness evidence.|A refreshed provisional view does not prove complete authoritative scope or business invariants.|A fresh file can be incomplete or semantically incorrect.|Healthy infrastructure does not establish correct final business output.\nLive and close paths calculate refunds differently. What design reduces divergence?|Versioned shared rules and tests|Two independently edited formulas|Only a larger live consumer group|Only a renamed final table|Semantic reuse and reviewed cases address the differing business calculation.|Independent undocumented rule changes preserve the risk of semantic divergence.|More consumers change capacity rather than aligning refund definitions.|Renaming output does not reconcile different business calculations.\nA batch repair appends all authoritative rows to the existing live table. What risk follows?|Duplicate business effects|Guaranteed exact replacement|Automatic deduplication without keys|Automatic source correction|Appending full scope can repeat already present events unless the sink enforces identity-aware semantics.|Appending full scope does not remove the previously stored copies.|Without identity-aware semantics, append does not automatically deduplicate replay.|Producer output append does not itself correct the original source data.\nA final generation changes a previously downloaded report. What is needed?|Consumer artifact correction|Only a new internal run id|Only restart the source parser|Only a faster next refresh|Previously distributed artifacts do not update merely because the producer now has correct data.|An internal run identity does not update an already downloaded report.|A parser restart does not deliver corrected bytes to the existing report consumer.|The prior downloaded artifact remains superseded even if new outputs arrive sooner.\nLive state has A=100,B=180; authoritative state has A=100,B=200,C=50. What explains the total increase?|B adds 20; C adds 50|50 correction for A plus 20 from C|70 from duplicating A|Only 20 from B|The total rises seventy through B's twenty-cent revision and C's fifty-cent addition.|A is unchanged and C is fifty, not twenty.|A remains one unchanged keyed value.|C also adds fifty to the population.\nA provisional total is 1000; late refunds reduce it by 80 and a missing sale adds 30. Final total?|950|1110|920|1030|Starting from 1000, subtract the late refund of 80 and add the missing sale of 30: 1000 - 80 + 30 = 950.|1110 adds the refund instead of subtracting.|920 omits the added sale.|1030 ignores the refund correction.\nA full close expects 100 keys. Candidate has 99 expected keys plus one unexpected key; total matches. What gate should reject it?|Two-way keys and invariants|Only total equality|Only candidate row count|Only max ingestion time|The candidate has a missing and substituted identity despite matching aggregates.|The total can be preserved by substitution.|One hundred rows does not prove the correct hundred keys.|Freshness does not establish identity coverage.\nA live path uses rule v2 and the close uses v3; source scope is identical but totals differ. Which release process makes a defensible final correction?|Explain, reconcile, version the close|Assume batch is always correct because it is slower|Relabel v3 as v2 without review|Append both totals so users can choose silently|A final correction needs an explained semantic version change and validated replacement behavior.|Execution mode alone does not prove correctness.|Relabeling destroys reproducible lineage.|Two unlabeled conflicting totals do not define a correct consumer contract.",
